# TradeVault - AI Project Guide

Read this guide before changing UI, adding a page, or introducing a component.

## Product and stack

TradeVault is a Forex trade-journaling and performance-management application. It helps users log trades, review execution quality, identify patterns, and improve their process. It is not a broker or trading terminal.

- Client: React 19, TypeScript, Vite, Tailwind CSS v4, React Router, Recharts, Lucide React, and React Icons.
- Server: Node.js, Express, Supabase PostgreSQL, and Supabase Auth.
- `client/src/pages/`: route-level views.
- `client/src/component/`: reusable UI grouped by feature.
- `client/src/context/`, `hooks/`, `services/`, and `types/`: shared app state, accessors, API calls, and types.
- `server/src/`: routes, controllers, middleware, services, and configuration.

`client/src/component/DashboardLayout.tsx` is the protected app shell. It provides sidebar, account, and trade context. Shell pages should use the shared `Navbar` and preserve its layout conventions.

## Design direction

The design was inspired by Vercel: quiet, high-contrast, minimal, functional, and product-focused. It should feel like a precise trading workstation, not a colorful consumer finance app.

### Font style

- Use Tailwind `font-sans` for standard interface text; it uses the native system sans stack.
- Use `font-mono` for metadata, compact stats, table headers, breadcrumbs, labels, and technical trading values.
- Use `font-semibold` or `font-bold` with `tracking-tight` for headings.
- Technical labels should use uppercase, `text-[10px]` or `text-[11px]`, `font-semibold`, and `tracking-widest` or `tracking-[0.2em]`.
- Body copy is normally `text-sm`; helper copy is `text-xs` and muted.
- Do not introduce a web font unless the entire product is intentionally rebranded.

### Color system

Use Tailwind zinc colors as the primary visual system. Avoid arbitrary colors for ordinary UI.

| Role | Preferred classes / color |
| --- | --- |
| Page canvas | `bg-black`, `#000000` |
| Main text | `text-white`, `text-zinc-100` |
| Strong surface | `bg-zinc-950`, `#050505` |
| Raised or selected surface | `bg-zinc-900` |
| Sidebar surface | `bg-[#121212]` |
| Standard border | `border-zinc-900` |
| Interactive/input border | `border-zinc-800` |
| Secondary text | `text-zinc-400` or `text-zinc-500` |
| Quiet/helper text | `text-zinc-600` or `text-zinc-700` |
| Positive P&L/status | `text-emerald-400`, subtle `emerald-500/20` border |
| Negative P&L/status | `text-rose-400` or `text-rose-500`, subtle `rose-500/20` border |
| Neutral/breakeven | `text-yellow-400` only when meaningful |

Use emerald and rose only for performance, outcomes, or destructive feedback. Do not use gradients, rainbow charts, saturated accent fills, or large colored backgrounds.

### Layout and interactions

- Use thin one-pixel zinc borders instead of heavy shadows or large colored cards.
- Use `rounded-md` for controls and `rounded-lg` for panels.
- Typical page spacing: `px-6 py-8` or `p-6`; major groups use `gap-6` to `gap-8`.
- Main content commonly uses `w-full max-w-7xl mx-auto`.
- Dense data uses mono labels and `divide-y divide-zinc-900`.
- Keep pages responsive: stack at small sizes, use multi-column grids at `sm`/`lg`.
- Standard controls: `border-zinc-800 bg-zinc-950 text-zinc-400`, hovering to `border-zinc-700 text-zinc-200` or `text-white`.
- Primary action: `bg-zinc-100 text-black`, hovering to white. It is intentionally neutral, not brand-blue.
- Inputs use dark backgrounds, `border-zinc-800`, and `focus:border-zinc-600`.
- Use subtle transitions, 14-17px icons, accessible labels, and visible focus styles.

## Implementation and data rules

1. Reuse `Navbar`, sidebar state, account state, and trade context instead of building replacements.
2. Start shell pages with `min-h-screen bg-black text-zinc-100 font-sans antialiased`.
3. Preserve existing account-aware routes. Dashboard, History, Calendar, and Analytics include account IDs; the journal is `/journal` and uses selected account context.
4. For account-scoped trade data, use `useTrade()` and `fetchTradesData(selectedAccount.id)`.
5. Match existing Tailwind classes and keep styles local unless a real global token is required.
6. Make empty, loading, error, and no-account states intentional and visually consistent.
7. Prefer actual trade data over static placeholders, and label derived or local-only data clearly.
8. Supabase Auth handles authentication; `AccountContext` persists the selected account; `TradeContext` owns cached trades and trade CRUD.
9. Trade Journal reflections are locally stored per account/date under `trade-journal:<account-id>:<YYYY-MM-DD>`. Do not describe them as server-synced unless persistence is added.

## Validation

For client changes, run from `client/`:

```bash
npm.cmd run build
```

On this Windows setup, PowerShell may block `npm`; use `npm.cmd`. Do not overwrite or revert unrelated dirty-worktree changes.
